A||N||5||Diamond Joe Quimby||Fan problem||||5||
Z||000000||Diamond Joe Quimby||06-12-2001||02:46 PM||||I have an 84 automatic (w/ ac if it matters), and I have a question about the radiator fan. When tracing the wiring from the fan relay to the fan motor, it goes to a quick disconnect, and then to a ceramic retangular bar before going to the fan motor. My question is, what is that ceramic bar? I think it is a rheostat, but I am not sure. Anyway, I took it out because I believed it to be bad, and it crumbled in my hands (now I know it is bad!). I need to replace it, but need to know what it is first. Any help would be greatly appreciated. A part number would be great if anyone knows it.<P>Thanks a ton,<BR>Jim||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||06-12-2001||04:27 PM||||I believe that's the resistor for the two speed fan. Has to dissapate tons of heat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-12-2001||07:10 PM||||Jim, hate to tell you this...<BR>I had to call Pontiac about that part. they don't sell it by itself. The only sell the whole setup. And it costs about $300...<BR>If I were you, I'd start calling a few junkyards.<BR>And terryk was right, that is a resistor. Not sure on the ohm value of it though.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||06-13-2001||07:44 PM||||If you saved the wire that went thru it you could use a meter to get the value. Then you could order an apropriate resistor from any number of electronics suppliers. A better choice would be an aluminum shell one. Something that can disipate many watts of power. I think the originals are 20watt but I've had no way to verify it....<P>For now just connect the ends of the insultated wire together and go without it. This will just make the fan run at full speed. It won't hurt anything.||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||06-13-2001||09:16 PM||||I changed my resistor about a year ago. Bought it from an electronics dealer as Ogre suggested, about $45 as I remember. I'll try to dig out the reciept tomorrow and see if there is a part # and some Rvalues and watts. It is an aluminum,ribbed resister and I had to drill a hole or two to mount it but it works fine. I'll post on this thread.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000005||Diamond Joe Quimby||06-14-2001||09:21 AM||||Hey guys, thanks for all the help. I figured I could count on you for some answers. I saved everything, so I will just go to an electronics store and pickup a resistor as Ogre suggested.<P>Don, thanks for looking for the receipt. If you get a part number, please let me know, and I will just get the same thing you did.<P>As for now, I will just hook it up as Ogre said and let it run full speed. It is getting warm here in MD, and it won't hurt anything to have the fan providing a little extra cooling!<P>thanks again all,<BR>jim<BR>||||reg||7
